1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,322
Opening the Financial Conversation.

2
00:00:02,322 --> 00:00:07,768
Most prospects have already been through
an underwriting process. They applied for

3
00:00:07,768 --> 00:00:12,676
an Amex Business Gold or a Capital One
card, and someone pulled their FICO

4
00:00:12,676 --> 00:00:17,719
score, asked for a personal guarantee,
and gave them a fraction of the limit

5
00:00:17,719 --> 00:00:22,426
they actually needed. That's their
reference point. So when you mention

6
00:00:22,426 --> 00:00:26,796
underwriting on a Dash-fi call, that's
the experience they expect.

7
00:00:26,796 --> 00:00:32,026
This is an opportunity to show them why
working with Dash-fi is different from

8
00:00:32,026 --> 00:00:37,459
anything they've experienced before — and
here's how the best reps on our team do

9
00:00:37,459 --> 00:00:37,663
it.

10
00:00:37,663 --> 00:00:42,121
**Tactic one: frame underwriting as
performance-based.**.

11
00:00:42,121 --> 00:00:45,418
Listen to how Heather Wilming opens this
conversation:

12
00:00:45,418 --> 00:00:52,784
"We do cash based or performance based
underwriting,

13
00:00:52,784 --> 00:01:00,056
meaning we can scale with you when you
are in your busy seasons or as you grow,

14
00:01:00,056 --> 00:01:07,515
because ultimately we give you access to
a quarter of what's in your bank account

15
00:01:07,515 --> 00:01:09,287
on a daily basis.".

16
00:01:09,287 --> 00:01:13,773
There are three tactics in that
positioning. She names the approach —

17
00:01:13,773 --> 00:01:18,787
"performance based." She connects it to
the prospect's growth — "we can scale

18
00:01:18,787 --> 00:01:23,537
with you." And she gives a concrete
number — "a quarter of what's in your

19
00:01:23,537 --> 00:01:28,023
bank account." The prospect now
understands that underwriting isn't a

20
00:01:28,023 --> 00:01:31,717
gate — it's how we figure out how much we
can offer them.

21
00:01:31,717 --> 00:01:37,725
Heather consistently uses this language,
and it shows in how her prospects engage

22
00:01:37,725 --> 00:01:43,057
with the process. Other AEs who have
adopted the same framing are seeing

23
00:01:43,057 --> 00:01:47,413
similar results — prospects engage
instead of pulling back.

24
00:01:47,413 --> 00:01:54,387
Nick Erdman frames it this way: "We do what
we call

25
00:01:54,387 --> 00:02:01,004
performance based underwriting where
we're looking at your revenue and bank

26
00:02:01,004 --> 00:02:07,978
balance, and that enables us to get you
guys limits that are five to 10X higher

27
00:02:07,978 --> 00:02:11,376
than the traditional card companies.".

28
00:02:11,376 --> 00:02:16,327
That's the kind of positioning that
changes a prospect's expectation of what

29
00:02:16,327 --> 00:02:18,110
underwriting can look like.

30
00:02:18,110 --> 00:02:22,475
**Tactic two: differentiate on
underwriting approach.**.

31
00:02:22,475 --> 00:02:27,742
When a prospect mentions their experience
with other card providers — personal

32
00:02:27,742 --> 00:02:32,599
guarantees, FICO pulls, low limits —
that's the opening to show them how

33
00:02:32,599 --> 00:02:37,456
Dash-fi's underwriting is different. Tim
Jonas uses this consistently to

34
00:02:37,456 --> 00:02:40,261
differentiate from traditional providers.

35
00:02:40,261 --> 00:02:48,918
"We underwrite based on company health. No

36
00:02:48,918 --> 00:02:55,818
personal guarantee, no FICO score,
anything like that.".

37
00:02:55,818 --> 00:02:58,512
Kurt Bell names the competition and the
problem:

38
00:02:58,512 --> 00:03:06,412
"Companies were trying to make a personal
guaranteed

39
00:03:06,412 --> 00:03:14,213
Amex or a Cap One card work and they just
didn't have high enough limits or the

40
00:03:14,213 --> 00:03:18,713
cash back rewards weren't aggressive
enough.".

41
00:03:18,713 --> 00:03:20,942
Cameron Baker adds specificity:

42
00:03:20,942 --> 00:03:31,373
"We don't require any UCCs or liens on the
business

43
00:03:31,373 --> 00:03:35,385
or even personal guarantees.".

44
00:03:35,385 --> 00:03:40,783
When a prospect hears "no personal
guarantee, no UCC, no FICO pull," it

45
00:03:40,783 --> 00:03:46,797
signals something deeper than product
features — it tells them Dash-fi is built

46
00:03:46,797 --> 00:03:52,195
for high-growth businesses, and our
underwriting process is designed to

47
00:03:52,195 --> 00:03:58,209
confirm mutual fit. It's not just that
we're a good fit for them. It's that our

48
00:03:58,209 --> 00:04:04,224
process validates they're a good fit for
us — and that distinction earns trust.

49
00:04:04,224 --> 00:04:10,687
**Tactic three: position Net-1 and a
general float option — let underwriting

50
00:04:10,687 --> 00:04:12,583
determine the rest.**.

51
00:04:12,583 --> 00:04:18,233
This is where deals can either close
quickly or get extended by weeks. When

52
00:04:18,233 --> 00:04:24,341
specific float terms are named up front —
"we can do Net-7, Net-15, Net-30" — the

53
00:04:24,341 --> 00:04:29,991
prospect anchors on a number. If the
underwriting team lands on a different

54
00:04:29,991 --> 00:04:35,107
term, you're now reselling a different
offer from a weaker position.

55
00:04:35,107 --> 00:04:41,947
Remember from Module 0: the underwriting
process for Net-1 is completely different

56
00:04:41,947 --> 00:04:48,281
from float terms. Net-1 requires only a
connected bank account, EIN, and KYC

57
00:04:48,281 --> 00:04:54,952
verification — and can be approved in 24
hours. Float terms — Net-15 or Net-30 —

58
00:04:54,952 --> 00:05:01,455
require 24 months of P&L and balance
sheets, plus a call with the underwriting

59
00:05:01,455 --> 00:05:07,957
team. That process takes at least three
business days. These are two different

60
00:05:07,957 --> 00:05:11,842
products with two different underwriting
paths.

61
00:05:11,842 --> 00:05:17,898
The principle is simple: underpromise and
overdeliver. Here's the positioning that

62
00:05:17,898 --> 00:05:19,319
protects your deal:

63
00:05:19,319 --> 00:05:24,692
"We offer a Net-1 option and a float
option. The exact float length is

64
00:05:24,692 --> 00:05:29,675
determined during underwriting based on
your financial profile.".

65
00:05:29,675 --> 00:05:33,984
Naming a specific term up front
pigeonholes the deal and risks

66
00:05:33,984 --> 00:05:38,999
disappointing the prospect if the
underwriting team lands on a different

67
00:05:38,999 --> 00:05:44,438
number. Keeping it general protects the
deal and lets the underwriting process

68
00:05:44,438 --> 00:05:46,487
work in the prospect's favor.

69
00:05:46,487 --> 00:05:51,879
You've positioned the card. You've shown
why Dash-fi's underwriting approach is

70
00:05:51,879 --> 00:05:56,787
different. And you've framed two clear
paths — Net-1 and float — without

71
00:05:56,787 --> 00:05:58,515
over-committing on terms.

72
00:05:58,515 --> 00:06:03,692
The next step is figuring out which path
fits this prospect, and the next section

73
00:06:03,692 --> 00:06:08,546
shows you how to ask those questions
without sounding like a credit analyst.
